<p>A shop report needs each item’s sales beside the shop’s total. An account history needs each event beside the amount accumulated so far. A latest-status query needs the newest event for every account. In each case, the calculation reaches into related rows while keeping the detail needed for the answer.</p>
<p>A <strong>window calculation</strong> adds that result to each input row. The important choices are which rows enter the calculation, which belong together, how they are ordered and which of them contribute at the current row. A short query can get any of those choices wrong and still return plausible numbers.</p>
<p>This follows <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#compare-rows-while-keeping-their-detail">Working with SQL’s first window query</a> and its <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#checkable-stages">named query stages</a>. We will use two small fixtures and <strong>PostgreSQL 18.6</strong>. All reported SQL results were checked on that native server. The figures calculate explanatory models of the same rows; their steps explain meaning, rather than showing the engine’s execution.</p>

